Keep the EditContext buffer where it is while the caret does not move.

After an Enter the keyboard types at the end of a line, the buffer keeps
its start on the line above, so that the offsets the keyboard holds stay
valid, and a word typed on the new line is composed in a two-line buffer.
When some line of the file wraps differently, as after a turn of the
screen in a file with a line wider than the screen, or when text that
the view draws inside a line arrives, such as a parameter hint, the view
sends the cursor again with its model position unchanged. The handler
took that for a move of the caret: it moved the buffer's start to the
caret's line and wrote the buffer again, with other text before the
caret and the caret at another offset in it. With no composition open,
Android told the keyboard that the selection moved and nothing else, and
Gboard 9.4, 10.8 and 12.4 then put the word they had composed in at the
caret again: `line two` became `line twotwo`, and Auto Save wrote it.
With a syllable open, the editor restarted input in its middle and the
next jamo began a new syllable: `f(한)` was saved as `f(하ㄴ)`.

A cursor event that leaves the primary selection where the buffer was
last built for, outside an edit of the keyboard's, now keeps the
buffer's start and the lines it holds after the selection's. Nothing is
written to the buffer then, so the keyboard keeps the copy it has. A
real move of the caret, a tap, a cursor key, a selection, a command that
moves the caret, a paste or an undo, still moves the buffer to the
caret's line as before, and an edit of the keyboard's still decides the
start as before, the bound on the lines before the caret included. Off
Android nothing changes.

This holds for a single caret only, and only when a single caret was
there as the buffer was built. A second caret on a line the buffer holds
types where the keyboard cannot see it, so the offsets before its own
caret change under it: with a caret added above by Add Cursor Above,
Gboard 15.9 replaced the line break with its next jamo. Adding, moving
or removing a caret therefore moves the buffer to the primary caret's
line as before, and with several carets nothing changes.

With several carets, an edit of the keyboard's first drops all but the
primary caret, and nothing is written to the buffer while they go: the
edit that follows rebuilds it and takes the count of carets it was built
for. Where none follows, in an editor that is read-only or where the
editor discards the edit, the count stayed at several, and the next
cursor event that left the caret in place moved the buffer to the
caret's line and restarted input. The count is taken when the carets are
dropped, because the buffer is the one the caret that stays was built
for.

An edit through the editor, a command's or the keyboard's, reaches the
content listener first, which writes the changed text in place at the
offsets the selection had; the cursor event comes after it, so the
existing restart for a changed text now runs when such an edit changes
the text of a kept buffer. It costs a restart of input, and the
keyboard's suggestions with it, where nothing told the keyboard of the
change before, and a syllable open at that moment ends there. For an
edit from another editor the cursor event comes first if it comes at
all, and then a selection has moved.

Source checks run the real cursor event handler, emit method,
composition and typing operations, with the model, the cursor list and
the EditContext replaced by adapters, on a host Node and not the Android
runtime; the adapters fire a cursor event after every edit that changes
the model, in the order the editor gives an edit through the cursor and
an edit from elsewhere. Replayed to the view's cursor event from 30
recordings of a word put in again at the caret, the code before this
change writes there what was recorded in 30 of 30, and with this change
nothing is written there. With several carets, constructed cases give
what the code before this change gives, the case recorded with Add
Cursor Above included, and every case with a single caret gives what it
gave with a single caret in an earlier form of this change. Four
deliberate breakages of the caret rule each fail some check. In a
read-only editor with several carets, a cursor event after the others
are dropped keeps the buffer; removing the line that takes the count
fails that case, and the other 108 named cases give the same with the
line as without it.

On devices, with a word typed on a new line in a file with a line wider
than the screen and a turn to landscape and back: on a build without
this change the word was doubled in 9 of 10 runs on an API 34 emulator
with Gboard 12.4 and WebView 157, in 5 of 10 on an API 33 emulator with
Gboard 12.4 and WebView 134, and in 7 of 10 on an API 30 emulator with
Gboard 9.4 and WebView 134. With an earlier form of this change, the
only difference between the two builds, it was doubled in 0 of 10 on the
first two. With the single-caret rule, on a later build that also
carries a later form of 0050 and other changes, among them the app
waiting for the keyboard's own commit before a turn ends the
composition, it was doubled in 0 of 10 on all three; in one of those
runs on the API 33 emulator the letter typed after the turn was written
over the word the keyboard had reopened. With a parameter hint arriving
in the middle of a Korean syllable, on an API 36 emulator with Gboard
15.9 (2-Bulsik) and WebView 134, `f(한)` came out wrong in 3 of 3 runs
without the change (`f(ㅎ하ㄴ)` twice, `f(ㅎㄴ)` once) and right in 3 of
3 with that earlier form and in 3 of 3 with the single-caret rule, the
syllable one composition from its first jamo to the commit. Two
syllables typed at two carets after Add Cursor Above, wrong in 2 of 2
runs with the earlier form, were right in 3 of 3 with the rule. The
count taken when the carets are dropped has the source checks only. The
keyboard can still write its word again by itself: on a later build with
this change, in one run in 40 on the API 33 emulator the line became
`line twotwo`, the keyboard inserting `two` at the caret and asking for
the caret to stay before it.

